<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tôn Đức Thắng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tôn Đức Thắng was a Vietnamese communist revolutionary who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> as president of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1969 and became the first president of the reunified Socialist Republic of Vietnam in 1976, holding office until his death. A former mechanic and labour organiser, he spent some sixteen years in French prisons, mostly on Poulo Condore, before 1945; the presidency under him was largely ceremonial.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ton-duc-thang.jpg" alt="Tôn Đức Thắng" width="203" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mr._Ton_Duc_Thang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tôn Đức Thắng was born on 20 August 1888 on Ông Hổ Island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, about four kilometres from Long Xuyên, to Tôn Văn Đề and Nguyễn Thị Di. From 1897 to 1901 a private tutor taught him Nôm script, history and Confucian philosophy, and the tutor's anti-colonial views influenced his early politics. He learned French at an elementary school in Long Xuyên and lived with his parents until 1906, when he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>. He later claimed to have taken part, as a sailor in the French navy, in the 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> mutiny, but the author Christoph Giebel says he apparently did not and calls the story a fabrication that linked Vietnamese communism to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Tôn joined the Vietnamese Revolutionary Youth League in 1927. In 1929 he was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and deported to the Côn Sơn Prison, where he stayed until 1945. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> came to power in August 1945 he joined the Cochinchina Party Committee, and in 1946 he presided over the National Assembly. He led the Lien Viet from 1946 to 1954 and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-fatherland-front">Vietnamese Fatherland Front</a>, and received the Stalin Peace Award in 1955. He was vice president under Hồ Chí Minh from 1960 and succeeded him as president on 2 September 1969. The post was ceremonial, he was never a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>, and most real power lay with the party chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a>.</p><p>He became the first president of the reunified Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, formalised on 2 July 1976, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975. Vietnamese forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> on 25 December 1978 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> by 7 January 1979, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> invaded northern Vietnam from 17 February 1979 and began to withdraw on 16 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tôn died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> on 30 March 1980, aged 91, of heart seizure and respiratory failure, and was succeeded by Nguyễn Hữu Thọ. He is buried in Mai Dịch Cemetery. Known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> as Uncle Tôn, he gave his name to Tôn Đức Thắng University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a>, and a museum devoted to him opened there in 1988, the centenary of his birth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tôn Đức Thắng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1976 the Fourth Party Congress renamed the ruling party the Communist Party of Vietnam and confirmed Le Duan as general secretary, with Pham Van Dong as prime minister and <b>Ton Duc Thang</b> as president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Long Xuyên, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.37,105.42&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Long Xuyên on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7456 1777 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tôn Đức Thắng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7906 2047h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7906 2047h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.37,105.42&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
